          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a migraine, and how is it different from an ordinary headache?",
          "answer": "A migraine is a recurring neurological condition. The headache is its most prominent feature, but it often comes with sensory sensitivity and nausea. Most people have occasional attacks, while a smaller group has them frequently. (Source: *Migraine*, General clinical reference)\n\nA typical migraine headache is often one-sided and pulsating, moderate to severe, and worsened by routine physical activity. It is often accompanied by nausea, vomiting, or sensitivity to light and sound. Untreated attacks commonly last from several hours to three days. (Source: *Migraine*)\n\nThe retrieved guidance also says not every attack has every feature, and the pain is on both sides of the head in a substantial share of people. Because of this, migraine is often mislabeled as an ordinary headache. The passage cuts off there, so I can't tell you more about how clinicians distinguish the two.\n\nIf you are wondering whether your own headaches are migraines, a doctor can assess your symptoms in person. Seek urgent care if you have a sudden, severe headache that is unlike any you've had before, or headache with weakness, confusion, trouble speaking, or vision loss.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
